\ Benchmark: body-entry cost in a program that loads no module.
\
\ This file imports nothing, and that is what it is for. A program that loads no module keeps
\ `quotation_scope_info` empty until the body-entry fill populates it. Every body entry afterwards
\ reads that map. A program that loads one has the map populated by capture regardless, so it never
\ had an empty map to lose.
\
\ The benchmark suite used to measure that class by accident. The rows that happened to be
\ importless acquired imports. This row holds the class on purpose, and
\ `make module-less-benchmark-check` fails if it grows an import.
\
\ An import is not the only way out of the class. A local binding makes a pushed quotation capture a
\ lexical frame, which populates the map by a route the fill has nothing to do with. So there are no
\ local bindings here either. No check sees that one.
\
\ Nearly all the time here is body entry. Every body is trivial and carries at least one
\ instruction. A first entry therefore publishes an entry and every later one reads as a hit, which
\ is the regime the class is actually in.
\
\ The loops are driven by `times` rather than `benchmark` or `benchmark-n`. Both of those point the
\ context's benchmark stats at their own for the whole timed region whatever the CLI flags say, and
\ that bookkeeping lands on the very path this file exists to measure. Driven by `times`, the same
\ file reports `timing.user_ns` under scripts/benchmark-ab.sh and runs un-instrumented under
\ scripts/benchmark-wall-ab.sh.
\
\ Run with --compile=off. A compiled body does not take the interpreter's body-entry path at all.

iters: ( -- fixnum ) const [ 400000 ] ;

bump: ( n: fixnum -- fixnum ) [ 1 + ] ;

sink: ( n: fixnum -- ) [ drop ] ;

word-chain: ( -- ) [ 0 bump bump bump bump bump sink ] ;

quot-chain: ( -- ) [ 0 [ bump ] call [ bump ] call [ bump ] call sink ] ;

run-words: ( -- ) [ iters [ word-chain ] times ] ;

run-quots: ( -- ) [ iters [ quot-chain ] times ] ;

run-words
run-quots
